{"id": "sevt_015bX9f7sZ4CLWqNzfU91gwp", "input": {"evidence": "Source: app/routes/export.py:11-12 registers the unauthenticated GET route /export (no auth decorator is visible in this file). app/routes/export.py:13 reads the attacker-controlled query parameter \"sort\" (default \"id\") and passes it through clean_identifier(). Sink: app/routes/export.py:15 interpolates the result straight into SQL with an f-string, \"SELECT * FROM reports ORDER BY {column}\", and runs it with sqlite3 conn.execute. This is not parameterized, and SQLite cannot bind identifiers as parameters anyway. Sanitizer: clean_identifier is imported from the external package acme_validation (app/routes/export.py:3), pinned at acme-validation==2.3.1 (requirements.txt:2). The package source is not in the repository: a search of /mnt/session/uploads finds no acme_validation module. Because of that, I can't confirm whether it enforces a strict identifier allowlist or regex (for example ^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$), only strips some characters, or can be bypassed. If it allows spaces, parentheses, commas, or keywords, ORDER BY injection is possible, for example a boolean or blind extraction through a CASE expression or subquery. Database: app/routes/export.py:14 connects to the SQLite DB at DB_PATH, which comes from config.py:3. Exploitability depends entirely on what clean_identifier does, so this is needs_review rather than a confirmed finding.", "finding_id": "F-107", "recommended_fix": "Don't rely on an external sanitizer for SQL identifiers. Check the sort value against an explicit allowlist of real reports columns and fall back to a safe default. For example: ALLOWED = {\"id\", \"created_at\", \"name\"}; col = request.args.get(\"sort\", \"id\"); if col not in ALLOWED: col = \"id\"; then build the query from the allowlisted value only. Optionally add a separate allowlisted ASC/DESC flag. As a review step, get the acme-validation 2.3.1 source and confirm how clean_identifier behaves. Also confirm whether /export should require authentication, close the connection (use a context manager), and add tests that send payloads like \"id DESC\", \"(SELECT 1)\", and \"id;--\".", "severity": "medium", "title": "Possible SQL injection in /export ORDER BY clause; depends on unverified third-party clean_identifier()", "verdict": "needs_review"}, "name": "create_ticket_draft", "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:55:32.574563Z", "type": "agent.custom_tool_use"}
