{"id": "sevt_014ki4hWJGrekMhKqvLaz9Zy", "content": [{"text": "I couldn't confirm or rule out this SQL injection, so I filed the ticket as **needs_review**. The repo doesn't include the code for the function that's supposed to sanitize the input.\n\n| ID | Rule | Location | Verdict | Severity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F-107 | SQL Injection | app/routes/export.py:15 | needs_review | medium |\n\n**What I found:**\n- The `/export` route (app/routes/export.py:11-12) has no visible login check. It takes the `sort` query parameter from the request (line 13) and pastes it straight into `ORDER BY {column}` with an f-string (line 15).\n- The only protection is `clean_identifier()`, which comes from the outside package `acme-validation==2.3.1` (requirements.txt:2). That package's code isn't anywhere in the uploads.\n- If `clean_identifier()` only accepts plain column names, this isn't exploitable. If it lets through spaces, brackets or subqueries, an attacker could pull data out one guess at a time through the sort order.\n\n**Recommended fix:** check `sort` against a fixed list of allowed column names in the route, and fall back to `id` for anything else. That closes the gap whatever `clean_identifier()` does. To settle the verdict, someone needs to read the acme-validation 2.3.1 source and check whether `/export` should require login.\n\n**Separate issue:** config.py:4 contains what looks like a hardcoded AWS secret key. I haven't repeated the value anywhere. If it's a real key, it should be rotated and moved out of the code into an environment variable or a secrets manager.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:55:38.878813Z", "type": "agent.message"}
